## What to deliver

Build and rig the Sapper up operation by operation until each `parts/<part>.png` matches
the brief, the assembled `scene/` previews show the parts fitting together correctly, and
the rig is complete: a tall, spindly, insectile obsidian creature on four thin high-kneed
legs, a narrow body held high on a long segmented spine curving up to a small head, two
long multi-jointed cutting shear arms at the front, a row of acid-green beads along the
spine, and a contiguous accent region on the outer faces of both shear arms sculpted
**only** in `#4a4358` and appearing nowhere else on the model. **Author the two required
animations** as F-curves with the `voxel-anim` animation subcommands: a **`move`** (a
quick, spidery, high-stepping stalk whose body stays high and level, authored in place)
and an **`attack`** (a cutting stroke where the shear arms open wide and scissor shut,
then reset, while the feet stay planted). The animations and the accent region are the
fixed contract — produce all of them; the parts and joints that realize them are yours.
You may add your own extra parts, joints, or animations on top, but do not drop or
contradict the required `move` and `attack`. Before you finish, run `voxel-anim render`
so it emits the per-part `.glb` geometry your result is built from — an unrendered part is
scored as empty (`voxel-anim render --component <part>` redraws one part; `voxel-anim
render --time <ms> --animation <name>` renders the model posed at that instant of an
animation to check the motion). When you are satisfied, stop: the recorded per-part logs
and `rig.json` are your finished submission.
